<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/hypoglycaemia> a dmo:AdverseEffect ;
    rdfs:label "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:adverseEffectCode "hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:frequencyCategory "Unknown" ;
    dmo:name "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Unknown" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below. Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia is the commonest side-effect of insulin or sulfonylurea therapy used to treat diabetes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "People with type 1 diabetes mellitus (T1DM) experience around two episodes of mild hypoglycaemia per week." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In unselected populations, the annual prevalence of severe hypoglycaemia has been reported consistently to be 30-40% in several large studies (11)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia was given as the cause of admission for 5% of admissions for people with T1DM and 1.5% of admissions for people with T2DM (17)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Over one third of 2000 ambulance call outs for severe hypoglycaemia represented repeat call outs, suggesting a significant minority are having recurrent problems (14)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The risk of hypoglycaemia with sulfonylurea therapy is often underestimated and due to the duration of action of these tablets, is frequently prolonged." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The UK Hypoglycaemia Group Study showed equivalent levels of severe hypoglycaemia in people with T2DM treated with sulfonylurea therapy compared with insulin therapy of less than two years duration (15)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "NaDIA (National Diabetes Inpatient Audit 2019) data shows a year on year reduction in the frequency of hypoglycaemic episodes experienced over the 7 days prior to the day of audit. However, 18.4% of people still reported one or more hypoglycaemic episodes (blood glucose <4.0mmol/L) with 7.0% reporting one or more hypoglycaemic episodes <3.0mmol/L." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "18.4% of people still reported one or more hypoglycaemic episodes (blood glucose <4.0mmol/L) with 7.0% reporting one or more hypoglycaemic episodes <3.0mmol/L." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "This may require oral or intravenous carbohydrate depending on the clinical situation; if the person is fasting for 30%" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

