<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "The hospital management of hypoglycaemia in adults with diabetes mellitus" ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005147" ;
    dmo:name "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The hospital management of hypoglycaemia in adults with diabetes mellitus" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia continues to be one of the most feared short-term complications of diabetes mellitus amongst people with diabetes, healthcare professionals and lay carers alike." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia is a serious condition and should be treated as an emergency regardless of level of conciousness. Hypoglycaemia is defined as blood sugar glucose of <4.0mmol/L (if not <4.0mmol/L but symptomatic give a small carbohydrate snack for symptom relief)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Treatment of hypoglycaemia algorithms\n\n11\n1. Quickly check the following.  Don’t spend too much time on this, particularly if the person" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "C. Adults who are unconscious and/or having seizures and/or are very aggressive" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below. Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If blood glucose remains less than 4.0mmol/L after 30-45 minutes (or 3 treatment cycles) give 100ml of 20% glucose at 400ml/hour or 200ml of 10% glucose at 800ml/hour over 15 minutes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia results from an imbalance between glucose supply, glucose utilisation and current insulin levels. Hypoglycaemia is the commonest side-effect of insulin or sulfonylurea therapy used to treat diabetes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia is a level of blood glucose below normal. Normal blood glucose levels in a person without diabetes are 3.5-7.0mmol/L. To avoid potential hypoglycaemia, Diabetes UK recommends a practical policy of “make four the floor”, i.e. 4.0mmol/L is the lowest acceptable blood glucose level in people with diabetes. In clinical practice, it can be defined as ”non-severe” if the episode is self-treated and “severe” if unable to self-treat and assistance by a third party is required (11)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia with a glucose <3.0mmol/L recurred in in 39% and 45% of people with T2DM and T1DM respectively, in a study of over 17,000 hospital admissions (20)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia can cause coma, hemiparesis and seizures. If the hypoglycaemia is prolonged the neurological deficits may become permanent." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "People with diabetes experiencing hypoglycaemia require a rapid-acting carbohydrate to restore euglycaemia (usually 4.0-7.0mmol/L)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Management of hypoglycaemia\n\n29\nComputerised approaches to reducing hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia requiring third party assistance and driving" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Glucose concentrations of less than 3.0 mmol/L (54 mg/dL) should be reported in clinical trials" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In patients with hypoglycemia is D50 too much of a good thing? J Emerg Med Serv. 2007;103–10." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypoglycaemia Audit Form To be completed by a Healthcare Professional after each hypoglycaemic episode" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Indication: Management of adult hypoglycaemia, where dose should be prescribed by volume and concentration to minimise confusion." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In all other situations, an infusion pump is required. Appendix 5" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hypoglycemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Hypoglycemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "hypoglycemia" ;
    dmo:definition "Hypoglycemia is a condition in which blood glucose levels fall below normal, leading to symptoms such as confusion, sweating, tremors, and in severe cases, loss of consciousness or seizures." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005040" ;
    dmo:name "Hypoglycemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The frequency and impact of hypoglycemia among hospitalized patients with diabetes: A population-based study." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/impaired-awareness-of-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Brain" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "impaired-awareness-of-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia (IAH) is an acquired syndrome associated with insulin treatment. IAH results in the warning symptoms of hypoglycaemia becoming diminished in intensity, altered in nature or lost altogether." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0019205" ;
    dmo:name "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Chronic" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia (IAH) is an acquired syndrome associated with insulin treatment. IAH results in the warning symptoms of hypoglycaemia becoming diminished in intensity, altered in nature or lost altogether." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/looming-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Looming hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "looming-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "The risk of looming hypoglycaemia should be considered in people who have a blood glucose level of 4.0-6.0mmol/L while in hospital." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0019075" ;
    dmo:name "Looming hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The risk of looming hypoglycaemia should be considered in people who have a blood glucose level of 4.0-6.0mmol/L while in hospital." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "A blood glucose value between 4.0 to 6.0mmol/L could indicate looming hypoglycaemia; particularly if repeated episodes showed a particular pattern of glucose measurements below 6.0mmol/L were occurring." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/nocturnal-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "nocturnal-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <3.9mmol/L) was more common in people treated with sulfonylurea therapy than insulin therapy (75.3% versus 59.3% respectively)." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0019070" ;
    dmo:name "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <3.9mmol/L) was more common in people treated with sulfonylurea therapy than insulin therapy (75.3% versus 59.3% respectively)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/severe-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Severe hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "severe-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "This increases the vulnerability of affected individuals of progression to severe hypoglycaemia." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0019206" ;
    dmo:name "Severe hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "This increases the vulnerability of affected individuals of progression to severe hypoglycaemia." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complicationStage/chronic-kidney-disease-stage-4> a dmo:ComplicationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Chronic Kidney Disease Stage 4" ;
    dmo:name "Chronic Kidney Disease Stage 4" ;
    dmo:stageCode "chronic-kidney-disease-stage-4" ;
    dmo:stageCriteria "due to the potassium content" ;
    dmo:stageOrder "4"^^xsd:integer ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "caution should be exercised in people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) stage 4 or CKD stage 5 due to the potassium content." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complicationStage/chronic-kidney-disease-stage-5> a dmo:ComplicationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Chronic Kidney Disease Stage 5" ;
    dmo:name "Chronic Kidney Disease Stage 5" ;
    dmo:stageCode "chronic-kidney-disease-stage-5" ;
    dmo:stageCriteria "due to the potassium content" ;
    dmo:stageOrder "5"^^xsd:integer ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "caution should be exercised in people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) stage 4 or CKD stage 5 due to the potassium content." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complicationStage/non-severe-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:ComplicationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Non-severe hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:name "Non-severe hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:stageCode "non-severe-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:stageCriteria "the episode is self-treated" ;
    dmo:stageOrder "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In clinical practice, it can be defined as ”non-severe\" if the episode is self-treated and “severe\" if unable to self-treat and assistance by a third party is required (11)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complicationStage/severe-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:ComplicationStage ;
    rdfs:label "Severe hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:name "Severe hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:stageCode "severe-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:stageCriteria "unable to self-treat and assistance by a third party is required" ;
    dmo:stageOrder "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In clinical practice, it can be defined as ”non-severe\" if the episode is self-treated and “severe\" if unable to self-treat and assistance by a third party is required (11)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/150-200ml-pure-fruit-juice> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "150-200ml pure fruit juice" ;
    dmo:contraCode "150-200ml-pure-fruit-juice" ;
    dmo:rationale "due to its potassium content" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "if following a low potassium diet (e.g. to treat chronic kidney disease) in view of its potassium content" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "150-200ml pure fruit juice (e.g. orange juice), do not use if following a low potassium diet (e.g. to treat chronic kidney disease) in view of its potassium content" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/1mg-of-glucagon-im> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "1mg of glucagon IM" ;
    dmo:contraCode "1mg-of-glucagon-im" ;
    dmo:rationale "may be less effective" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "when administered repeatedly, in people prescribed sulfonylurea therapy or people with a history of alcohol abuse or chronic liver disease" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "1mg of glucagon IM (only licensed for insulin induced hypoglycaemia, may be less effective when administered repeatedly, in people prescribed sulfonylurea therapy or people with a history of alcohol abuse or chronic liver disease)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/50-glucose-solution> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "50% glucose solution" ;
    dmo:contraCode "50-glucose-solution" ;
    dmo:rationale "50% glucose solution is a hypertonic solution with the potential to cause significant tissue damage from extravasation and even skin necrosis. Phlebitis increases the risk of extravasation as it causes endothelial damage making the vein more permeable or even leading to rupture." ;
    dmo:severity "Absolute" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "extravasation" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Fifty percent glucose solution is a hypertonic solution with the potential to cause significant tissue damage from extravasation and even skin necrosis.  Phlebitis increases the risk of extravasation as it causes endothelial damage making the vein more permeable or even leading to rupture (65)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The potential risks have led to recommendations that 50% glucose should only be administered through central venous access; clearly this may not be a practical recommendation, particularly in the context of emergency treatment." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Fifty percent glucose also has a pH range of 3.2-6.5.  These two factors are thought to increase the risk of phlebitis and therefore extravasation." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/acute-discontinuation-of-long-term-corticosteroid-therapy> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Acute discontinuation of long-term corticosteroid therapy" ;
    dmo:contraCode "acute-discontinuation-of-long-term-corticosteroid-therapy" ;
    dmo:rationale "Acute discontinuation of corticosteroids can precipitate hypoglycaemia due to reduced counterregulatory response" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Acute discontinuation of long-term corticosteroid therapy" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Acute discontinuation of long-term corticosteroid therapy" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/caution-in-people-receiving-anticoagulation> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Caution in people receiving anticoagulation" ;
    dmo:contraCode "caution-in-people-receiving-anticoagulation" ;
    dmo:rationale "due to the bleeding risk following an intramuscular injection" ;
    dmo:severity "Caution" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "people receiving anticoagulation" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Caution should also be exercised in people receiving anticoagulation due to the bleeding risk following an intramuscular injection." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/change-of-insulin-injection-site> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Change of insulin injection site" ;
    dmo:contraCode "change-of-insulin-injection-site" ;
    dmo:rationale "Changing insulin injection sites can affect absorption and increase risk of hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Change of insulin injection site" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Change of insulin injection site" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/chocolate-for-hypoglycaemia-treatment> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Chocolate for Hypoglycaemia Treatment" ;
    dmo:contraCode "chocolate-for-hypoglycaemia-treatment" ;
    dmo:rationale "Chocolate contains rapid-acting carbohydrate and fat; the addition of fat has been shown to slow the absorption of quick-acting carbohydrate" ;
    dmo:severity "Absolute" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Use of chocolate for treating hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Chocolate is no longer recommended for the treatment of hypoglycaemia.  Chocolate contains rapid-acting carbohydrate and fat; the addition of fat has been shown to slow the absorption of quick-acting carbohydrate (50,51)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/concurrent-use-of-medicines-with-hypoglycaemic-agents> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Concurrent use of medicines with hypoglycaemic agents" ;
    dmo:contraCode "concurrent-use-of-medicines-with-hypoglycaemic-agents" ;
    dmo:rationale "Concurrent use of medicines with hypoglycaemic agents can precipitate hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Concurrent use of medicines with hypoglycaemic agents e.g. warfarin, quinine, salicylates, fibrates, sulphonamides (including cotrimoxazole), linezolid, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), probenecid, somatostatin analogues, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Concurrent use of medicines with hypoglycaemic agents e.g. warfarin, quinine, salicylates, fibrates, sulphonamides (including cotrimoxazole), linezolid, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), probenecid, somatostatin analogues, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors. Do not stop or withhold medication, discuss with the medical team or pharmacist" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/confusing-the-insulin-name-with-the-dose> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Confusing the insulin name with the dose" ;
    dmo:contraCode "confusing-the-insulin-name-with-the-dose" ;
    dmo:rationale "Confusing the insulin name with the dose can lead to incorrect insulin administration and hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Confusing the insulin name with the dose (e.g. Humalog Mix25 becoming Humalog 25 units)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Confusing the insulin name with the dose (e.g. Humalog Mix25 becoming Humalog 25 units)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/endocrine-disorders-associated-with-diminished-counterregulatory-responses> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses" ;
    dmo:contraCode "endocrine-disorders-associated-with-diminished-counterregulatory-responses" ;
    dmo:rationale "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses can precipitate hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses (e.g. Addison’s disease, growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses (e.g. Addison’s disease, growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/failure-to-monitor-blood-glucose-adequately> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Failure to monitor blood glucose adequately" ;
    dmo:contraCode "failure-to-monitor-blood-glucose-adequately" ;
    dmo:rationale "Inadequate monitoring increases the risk of undetected hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "Failure to monitor blood glucose adequately" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Failure to monitor blood glucose adequately" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/glucagon-in-people-with-alcohol-abuse> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Glucagon in people with alcohol abuse" ;
    dmo:contraCode "glucagon-in-people-with-alcohol-abuse" ;
    dmo:rationale "Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease." ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "abuse alcohol" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/glucagon-in-people-with-chronic-liver-disease> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Glucagon in people with chronic liver disease" ;
    dmo:contraCode "glucagon-in-people-with-chronic-liver-disease" ;
    dmo:rationale "Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease." ;
    dmo:severity "Relative" ;
    dmo:triggerCriteria "have chronic liver disease" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_01_hypo_guideline_with_qr_code_january_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/glucagon-in-people-with-chronic-malnutrition> a dmo:Contraindication ;
    rdfs:label "Glucagon in people with chronic malnutrition" ;
    dmo:contraCode "glucagon-in-people-with-chronic-malnutrition" ;
    dmo:rationale "Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease." ;
